## Technology Stack

* **React Native** — cross-platform application framework
* **Expo** — development and Android application tooling
* **TypeScript** — application development and type safety
* **AsyncStorage** — persistent local application data
* **React Navigation** — application navigation and screen management
* **Android** — initial production platform

## Application Architecture

Meal Mastery is organized around several connected areas of application state:

```text
User Preferences
       │
       ▼
Meal Generator ◄──── Recipe Library
       │
       │
       ├──────────────► 7-Day Meal Plan
       │                       │
       │                       ▼
       │                 Shopping List
       │
       ▼
Pantry Inventory
       │
       ├── Ingredient Availability
       └── Expiry Awareness
```

The goal is to connect information that is often managed separately.

A recipe is not useful simply because it exists in a database. It becomes more useful when the application understands what the user wants to eat, what ingredients are already available, what should be used soon, and what still needs to be purchased.

## Designing the Meal Generator

One of the more interesting parts of Meal Mastery was designing meal generation as more than random recipe selection.

The generator has to balance several pieces of information:

* user preferences
* recipe availability
* pantry contents
* ingredient matching
* ingredient expiry
* variety across the generated plan

This meant treating meal selection as a scoring and filtering problem rather than simply choosing an item from an array.

That distinction became increasingly important as the recipe library grew.

## Local-First Data

Meal Mastery uses local persistent storage for application information such as preferences, pantry state, meal plans, and shopping-list data.

For this type of application, a local-first approach provides several benefits:

* fast access to frequently used data
* continued functionality without constant network access
* reduced infrastructure requirements
* simpler handling of personal pantry and planning information

AsyncStorage provides persistence between application sessions while keeping the architecture appropriate for the project's scope.

## Connecting Pantry Data to Recommendations

The pantry became one of the most useful parts of the application because it could influence other features instead of existing as an isolated inventory list.

Pantry information can contribute to recipe selection by identifying meals that already match ingredients the user owns.

Expiry awareness adds another signal.

If multiple meals are otherwise suitable, using an ingredient approaching its expiry can make one recipe more useful than another.

This creates a simple principle behind the recommendation system:

**The best meal isn't necessarily the highest-rated recipe. It's the meal that best fits the user's current situation.**

## From Meal Plan to Shopping List

Generating meals solves only part of the planning problem.

The next question is:

**What do I need to buy?**

Meal Mastery connects the generated plan with shopping-list functionality so users can move from choosing meals to preparing for the week without rebuilding the plan manually.

This was an important product-design lesson: useful software often comes from connecting adjacent steps in a workflow rather than optimizing one feature in isolation.

## Building Cook Mode

Cooking presents a different user experience from planning.

When someone is actively preparing food, they don't need the same information density or navigation options they need while organizing the week.

Cook Mode was designed around that context, providing a more focused way to use recipe information during preparation.

It reinforced an important lesson from building the application:

**The same user can have very different interface requirements depending on what they are trying to accomplish at that moment.**

## Shipping the Application

Meal Mastery progressed beyond a development prototype and was released as an Android application.

Moving from development to release introduced a different set of considerations from simply implementing features.

The application needed to behave consistently across sessions, preserve user data, provide understandable navigation, and function as a complete product rather than a collection of individual screens.

Shipping the application also changed how I approached development.

Once software reaches actual users, reliability, usability, upgrade behavior, and seemingly small interface decisions become much more important.

## What I Learned

### Build workflows, not isolated features

The strongest parts of Meal Mastery emerged when individual features began working together.

Pantry tracking became more valuable when it influenced meal generation. Meal generation became more valuable when it created a weekly plan. The weekly plan became more useful when it connected to shopping and cooking.

### Data structure affects product capability

How recipes, ingredients, preferences, and pantry items are represented directly determines what the application can reason about later.

Features such as ingredient matching and expiry-aware recommendations depend heavily on consistent underlying data.

### Simple automation can provide meaningful value

A recommendation system does not need to be unnecessarily complicated to be useful.

Combining several understandable signals can produce results that feel substantially smarter than random selection.

### Shipping teaches different lessons than prototyping

Building a feature until it works locally is only one part of software development.

Turning those features into something another person can install and use creates a much higher standard for consistency and completeness.

### Product decisions are engineering decisions

Choices about navigation, persistence, data models, and feature interaction affect both the user experience and the technical architecture.

Building Meal Mastery gave me practical experience making those tradeoffs rather than treating application development as a collection of independent coding tasks.

## Project Status

Meal Mastery is a functional Android application and an ongoing personal software-development project.

Future areas of exploration include expanding the recipe library, improving recommendation logic, refining pantry intelligence, and continuing to improve the planning and cooking experience.

## About the Project

Meal Mastery began as an idea for making an everyday problem easier through software.

Building it gave me the opportunity to work across the complete application lifecycle: defining the problem, designing features, structuring data, implementing the application in TypeScript and React Native, testing workflows, iterating on the user experience, and ultimately shipping a usable Android application.

For me, that end-to-end process is the most valuable part of the project.

**The goal wasn't simply to build an app. It was to build something people could actually use.**
